  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Biodiversity and Community Resilience in the Omo Valley</narrative>
  </title>
  <description>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">(Ethiopia):  The Biodiversity and Community Resilience in the Omo Valley (BIOM) activity increases the resilience of communities and the ecosystem of South Omo, which has undergone a series of major shocks in recent decades. South Omo communities have been affected by major displacements due to the  Gibe III dam construction, expanding export agriculture, and escalating conflicts between the state and local groups. The activity will work with indigenous communities to increase their capacity to sustainably manage their biodiversity and to develop community-managed and led approaches to sustainable livelihoods. The activity will improve outcomes for communities by centering them (particularly women and youth) as stewards of the landscape and key organizers of conservation efforts. It will also train and empower locals to engage more directly in the tourist economy. Through privately funded, community-run ecotourism ventures in Tama Wildlife Sanctuary, a Community Conserved Area (CCA), the activity will promote conservation of local biodiversity and increase tourism-based income. BIOM will reduce threats to high biodiversity areas, such as the Omo and Mago National Parks, by promoting diversification of livelihoods and improving access to basic social and economic services. The formalization of the CCA will promote land rights and tenure security for local communities who experience tensions with migrant laborers working in adjacent sugar cane plantations...... .. .</narrative>
  </description>
